From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 04:52:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: say in a suspend's reply whether it synced file systems

The host asks the guest to sync, trims it and then suspends it, and
counts the storage as settled from the sync's reply alone, because
the suspend's reply, "suspended", says only that the guest reached
the sleep boundary: the PM core syncs on the way only while
sync_on_suspend is set, and the reply did not say whether it did.

Reply "suspended, synced" when the suspend's own sync ran to its
return before this reply, and "suspended, not synced" otherwise (sync
disabled).  A suspend that failed or was given up replies as before,
with an error and no statement.  Both texts still contain "suspended"
and carry no error flag, which is all an older host looks for, so it
takes them as it took the old reply.  A kernel without this change
replies "suspended", which says nothing, and the host keeps treating
that as nothing confirmed.

Only the task that runs the request's pm_suspend() makes the
statement.  The request is claimed before that task has the PM
transition mutex, so a suspend the guest began earlier, already past
its sync, can reach the enter callback first and find the request; its
sync may predate the host's request.  Such a suspend replies
"suspended".  The requester is recorded after the claim and cleared
before the request is released, and the console runs its requests one
after the other, so a stale requester never meets a later request.

The request's retries stop once the request is gone: the guest's own
suspend that answered it has the guest asleep for the host's wake, and
a retry that began another suspend after the first one ended would put
the guest to sleep with nobody waiting to wake it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c | 45 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c
index d9753c60ac429c5b1fdb3a556c38934c89112357..8ea8da2987580eab09eb6eb8ba042db5821115f6 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c
@@ -222,7 +222,9 @@ void mconsole_proc(struct mc_request *req)
     cad - invoke the Ctrl-Alt-Del handler \n\
     stop - pause the UML; it will do nothing until it receives a 'go' \n\
     go - continue the UML after a 'stop' \n\
-    suspend - enter system suspend until the host sends 'wake' \n\
+    suspend - enter system suspend until the host sends 'wake'; replies\n\
+        'suspended, synced' or 'suspended, not synced', by whether the\n\
+        suspend synced the file systems \n\
     wake - resume a system-suspended UML \n\
     log <string> - make UML enter <string> into the kernel log\n\
     proc <file> - returns the contents of the UML's /proc/<file>\n\
@@ -264,6 +266,11 @@ void mconsole_go(struct mc_request *req)
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_SUSPEND
 static struct mc_request *suspend_request;
+/*
+ * The task that runs pm_suspend() for suspend_request: set after the request
+ * is claimed, and cleared before it is released.
+ */
+static struct task_struct *suspend_requester;
 /* A wake arrived while the host's suspend request was in progress. */
 static bool suspend_woken;
 /* The wake requests answered so far; only a change between two reads counts. */
@@ -300,12 +307,35 @@ static bool mconsole_overtaken(const struct mc_request *req)
 	return atomic_read(&mconsole_wakes) != req->wakes;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The host's suspend is answered once the guest has reached the sleep
+ * boundary, after the PM core's own sync, which it does only while
+ * sync_on_suspend is set (pm_suspend_synced()).  "suspended, synced" says
+ * that sync returned for this suspend; "suspended, not synced" says there was
+ * none.  Tasks are frozen after the sync, so a write between the two is not
+ * covered.
+ *
+ * Only the task that runs the request's pm_suspend() states it.  The request
+ * is claimed before that task has the PM transition mutex, so a suspend the
+ * guest began earlier can enter first and find the request: its sync may
+ * predate the host's request and say nothing of what the host asked, so it
+ * answers "suspended", as does a kernel without this statement, which the
+ * host takes as nothing confirmed.  The console runs its requests one after
+ * the other and clears the requester before it releases the request, so a
+ * stale requester never meets a later request.
+ */
 void um_suspend_notify_entered(void)
 {
 	struct mc_request *req = xchg(&suspend_request, NULL);
 
-	if (req != NULL)
+	if (req == NULL)
+		return;
+	if (READ_ONCE(suspend_requester) != current)
 		mconsole_reply(req, "suspended", 0, 0);
+	else
+		mconsole_reply(req, pm_suspend_synced() ?
+			       "suspended, synced" : "suspended, not synced",
+			       0, 0);
 }
 
 int um_suspend_wake_requested(void)
@@ -355,16 +385,25 @@ void mconsole_suspend(struct mc_request *req)
 	 * one of them sees it, in whichever order the requests run.
 	 */
 	if (!mconsole_overtaken(req)) {
+		WRITE_ONCE(suspend_requester, current);
 		error = pm_suspend(PM_SUSPEND_MEM);
 		while (error == -EBUSY && retries++ < MCONSOLE_SUSPEND_RETRIES &&
 		       !READ_ONCE(suspend_woken) &&
 		       READ_ONCE(suspend_request) != NULL) {
 			msleep(MCONSOLE_SUSPEND_RETRY_MS);
-			if (READ_ONCE(suspend_woken))
+			/*
+			 * A suspend of the guest's own may have taken the
+			 * request at its entry meanwhile, and the host has its
+			 * answer: another suspend would put the guest to sleep
+			 * with nobody waiting to wake it.
+			 */
+			if (READ_ONCE(suspend_woken) ||
+			    READ_ONCE(suspend_request) == NULL)
 				break;
 			error = pm_suspend(PM_SUSPEND_MEM);
 		}
 	}
+	WRITE_ONCE(suspend_requester, NULL);
 	pending = xchg(&suspend_request, NULL);
 	WRITE_ONCE(suspend_woken, false);
 	if (pending == NULL)
